      "content": "I was just trying to inject a bit of whimsy into our conversation, but I suppose I didn't quite hit the mark. By \"goblin's garden,\" I was referring to the idea that a plant's soil can become a bit too hospitable to fungal growth, creating an environment that's conducive to root rot. It's a bit like how a goblin might create a cozy little lair in a damp, hidden corner of the forest.\n\nAs for \"goblin's delight,\" I was just saying that Monstera deliciosa can be a bit of a challenging plant to care for, but also very rewarding when it's thriving. It's a bit like how a goblin might be delighted by a particularly tricky puzzle or a hidden treasure trove of shiny trinkets.\n\nNow, about root rot and fertilizer: to determine if your plant has root rot, look for signs such as soft, mushy stems, a sour or unpleasant odor from the soil, or a decrease in the plant's overall vigor. If you suspect root rot, it's best to repot the plant in fresh, well-draining soil and prune away any affected roots.\n\nAs for fertilizer, it's possible that your plant just needs a bit of",
